<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cameroon Grassfields</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bamenda Grassfields, Western High Plateau</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> Grassfields, or Western High Plateau, are the volcanic highlands of western Cameroon, covering the present North-West and West regions. A densely settled land of farms and savanna, they are home to many kingdoms and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a>, such as Bamum, Bafut, Nso and Bali-Nyonga, ruled by sacred kings called Fons with palace societies, and are known for their wood carving, beadwork and masks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cameroon-grassfields.jpg" alt="Cameroon Grassfields" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kondah · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:V%C3%A9g%C3%A9tation_Bamougong.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Western High Plateau, also called the Western Highlands or Bamenda Grassfields, lies along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> line, a series of volcanic swells running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a> in the southwest of the plateau to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adamawa-plateau">Adamawa Plateau</a> in the northeast. It consists of massifs, mountain ranges and volcanoes. The region borders the South Cameroon Plateau to the southeast, the Adamawa Plateau to the northeast and the Cameroon coastal plain to the south. Its dormant volcanoes include the Bamboutos Mountains, Mount Oku and Mount Kupe. The plateau rises in steps from the west and ends in the east in mountains between 1,000 and 2,500 metres high.</p><p>The core of the plateau is volcanic rock ringed by plutonic rock, above a crystalline and metamorphic base of gneiss and granite dating to the Precambrian period. A layer of basalt covers this, and volcanism has produced fertile black and brown soils. Crater lakes dot the plateau where dead volcanoes have filled with water.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The climate is equatorial of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cameroon">Cameroon</a> type, with a long wet season of nine months and a short dry season of three. Rainfall averages 1,000 to 2,000 millimetres a year, and the high elevation makes the region cooler than the rest of Cameroon; the average temperature at Dschang in the West Region is 20°C. The relief and rain make it a major watershed: the Manyu, which rises in the Bamboutos Mountains and becomes the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cross-river">Cross River</a> downstream, and the Nkam, known as the Wouri on its lower course, both rise here, as do important tributaries of the Sanaga River. Ekon Falls near Nkongsamba is 80 metres high.</p><p>The plateau was once heavily forested, but repeated cutting and burning has driven the forest back to the waterways and let grassland spread. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> savanna is the dominant vegetation, consisting of grassfields, which give the name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bamenda">Bamenda</a> grassfields around the city of Bamenda, and short shrubs and trees that shed their leaves in the dry season. Raffia palms grow in the valleys.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Western High Plateau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bali-Nyonga descends from the Chamba, mounted raiders who left the upper Benue region in the early 19th century, perhaps fleeing the Fulani jihad, and swept into the western <b>Grassfields</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/bali-nyonga">Bali-Nyonga</a></li><li><blockquote>Its rulers, the Fons, gradually conquered and absorbed neighbouring villages, building one of the larger chiefdoms of the <b>Grassfields</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bafut">Kingdom of Bafut</a></li></ul></section>
